# Chapter 2, part 2: the commands Save and Retrieve.
.h2 Save [file-save]
.ix Save
.ix Save File panel
.ix Ctrl-S
.ix Saving a document
.ix Save As
.ix F12, File, Save
.ix Description, Save panel
.ix Author, Save panel
.ix Revision, Save panel
.ix Overwrite old version on disk
Save writes the document, or a part of it, to a file.  Until a document is
saved it exists only in the memory of the Editor.  The name on the panel can
be changed, so Save also does what other programs call Save As, and its
Options field makes templates and extracts.  {k:Ctrl-S} writes the document at
once under its own name and shows no panel.
.table 20 40
Way to reach it | Notes
{k:F12}, File, Save | Press {k:F12} and then {k:F} for File
 | and {k:S} for Save.  The keys {k:Left} and
 | {k:Right} and {k:Enter} do the same.  The panel
 | Save File opens.
{k:Ctrl-S} | Saves under the name of the document, at once.
 | See {x:file-save-quick}.
.end
The menu hint on the line below the menu bar reads "Save the current document
on the disk [{k:Ctrl-S}]".
.pic pictures/file-save.png 5 The Save File panel.
.h3 The Save File panel [file-save-panel]
.ix Save File, fields
.ix Document Name, Save
.ix Options, Save
The panel has six fields.  The keys that move between them and accept the
panel are those of {x:file-panels}.
.table 15 45
Field | Meaning and value
Working Directory | The directory that the file is written in.  It is
 | shared by all the File panels
 | ({n:file-workdir}).  {k:F2} lists the directories.
 | Default: the working directory.
Document Name | The name of the file.  The field begins with the name
 | of the document, without its directory.  A name with
 | no dot gets the extension {c:.doc}, or {c:.set} when
 | the option is Save Global Settings Only
 | ({n:file-names-ext}).  {k:F2} lists the files that end
 | with {c:.doc}, {c:.set} or {c:.bak} (and a number).  The field must
 | not be empty.
Options | What is written: Save Entire Document, Save Global
 | Settings Only or Extract Current Section (Extract
 | Current Block when the document is not structured).
 | Default: Save Entire Document.
Description | The description of the document.  It begins with the
 | description that the document has, or is empty.
Author | The author of the document.  It begins with the author
 | that the document has, or is empty.
Revision | The revision number, in the form {c:1.007}.  It begins
 | with the revision that the document has.
.end
All the text fields are edited as {x:file-panels-text} says, and
Options is changed as {x:file-panels-choice} says.  A document that
is new has the revision 1.000, an empty description and an empty author unless
the New panel or the Document Manager gave it some.
.h3 What the Options field writes [file-save-options]
.ix Save Entire Document
.ix Save Global Settings Only
.ix Extract Current Section
.ix Extract Current Block
.ix Template, Save Global Settings Only
.ix Extract, section
.pic pictures/file-save-options.png 4 The list of the Options field of the Save panel.
.table 22 38
Value | What the file holds
Save Entire Document | The whole document: the settings and every
 | section and block of the text.  The document takes
 | the name and the directory of the file, and it is no
 | longer marked as changed.  It is the only value
 | that makes backups ({n:file-backup-when}).
Save Global Settings Only | All the settings, including the description,
 | the author and the revision, and no text at all.
 | The result is a template.  The name gets {c:.set}
 | when it has no dot.  The document in
 | the Editor keeps its name and its text.
Extract Current Section | A complete document that holds the section the
 | cursor is in with all its subsections, and the
 | settings of the document.  The document
 | in the Editor is not changed and keeps its name.
Extract Current Block | The same for a document that is not structured: the
 | block the cursor is in.
.end
The file of Extract Current Section is a valid document of its own.  The
extracted section is the first section of the new document, whatever its
number was in the old one: the section 2.1.2 of a report becomes the section 1
of the extract.  The extract has no title page.  Opening it later is an
ordinary Retrieve.
.note The settings of a template are all the settings of the document at that
  moment: the global formats, the page layouts, the typefaces, the marker
  settings, the description, the author and the revision.  A template
  made by Save Global Settings Only has no text, no title page, no macros and
  no libraries.  A template with starting text is made with Save Entire
  Document under a name that ends with {c:.set} ({n:file-templates-create}).
.h3 Description, Author and Revision [file-save-dar]
.ix Description, change
.ix Author, change
.ix Revision, rule at Save
What is typed in the fields Description and Author becomes the description and
the author of the document, and is written in the file.  They
are the values that the markers Description and Author print.  Changing
either of them counts as changing the document: the revision goes up even if
no text was edited ({n:file-revision-rule} gives the rule).  A field
that is left as it was changes nothing.
.pic pictures/file-overwrite.png 4 The question that is asked when the file exists.
The Revision field is read when the panel is accepted.  The rule is this.
.table 33 27
What is in the field | What is written
The number shown on the panel, and the | The number plus 0.001.
 document or its description or author | (1.004 becomes 1.005.)
 was changed |
The number shown on the panel, and | The number shown.
 nothing was changed |
Any other number | That number, as typed.
.end
A number that is typed is accepted if it has digits, an optional point and
at most three digits after the point: {c:3}, {c:2.5} and {c:.25} are numbers;
{c:1.2345}, {c:-1}, {c:1.2a} and an empty field are not, and the panel shows
"Not a valid revision number" ({n:file-panels-check}).  A number
typed that happens to be equal to the number shown counts as not typed: the
automatic rule applies.
.caution Save Global Settings Only and Extract Current Section apply the
  revision rule to the document in the Editor too.  If the document has been
  changed, the revision in the Editor goes up by 0.001 as the file is written,
  although the document itself was not saved and is still marked as changed.
  The next Save then shows a revision that is one higher than the document had
  on the disk.  Typing the revision on the Save panel avoids the surprise.
.h3 The question and the result [file-save-result]
.ix Overwrite old version on disk?
.ix Save, existing file
.ix Save failed
.ix Saved (message)
When the panel is accepted the Editor first tests the name.  If a file of
that name exists, whatever the option is and even if it is the file of the
document itself, the question "Overwrite old version on disk?" is asked, with
the entries Yes and No ({n:file-panels-yn}); No leaves the panel's
work undone and the document unchanged, except that the Working Directory has
been remembered.  Then, in this order:
.table 4 57
Step | What happens
1 | The description and the author are put in the document, if they were
 | changed, and the revision is set by the rule above.
2 | With Save Entire Document, Backups Kept above 0 and a file of that name
 | on the disk that the new version differs from, the backups are moved up
 | and the file on the disk becomes backup 1 ({n:file-backup-when}).
3 | The file is written.
4 | If it was written, the message line shows "Saved" and the full name of
 | the file, for example "Saved" and the path.  With Save Entire
 | Document the document takes that name and loses its changed mark; the
 | status line no longer shows the asterisk.
5 | If it was not written, a box with the title "Save failed" shows the
 | reason (see the table below), the revision and the changed mark are put
 | back as they were, and the document keeps its old name.
.end
The reasons that the box can give:
.table 29 31
Reason | Cause
cannot write file | The file could not be opened for writing (Save
 | Entire Document): the directory does not exist, is
 | not writable, or a name of that kind is a directory.
cannot write {i:name} | The same for Save Global Settings Only and Extract
 | Current Section.  The name is the full path.
A backup file is not saved over: use Restore Backup or give another name | The
 | name is that of a backup ({n:file-backup-use}).
write error | The file was opened but writing or closing it failed
 | (the disk is full, for instance).
There is no current section | Extract Current Section was chosen and the cursor
 | is in no section.
.end
A save that fails leaves the document on the disk as it was: the new version
is written completely before it replaces the old one, and the changes stay in
the Editor.  The backups, if there are any, hold the versions before.
.h3 Ctrl-S, the quick save [file-save-quick]
.ix Quick save
.ix Ctrl-S, quick save
.ix Save without a panel
.ix Autosave, quick save
{k:Ctrl-S} saves the document at once.  No panel is used and no question is
put: Save Entire Document is written under the name that the document has,
in the directory of that name.  The rules are these.
.table 31 29
Situation | What {k:Ctrl-S} does
The document has a name | Writes the file, without asking.
The document has changed | The revision goes up by 0.001 first.
The document has not changed | The revision stays; nothing is written and
 | no backup is made ({n:file-backup-when}).
Backups Kept is above 0 | Makes the backups first
 | ({n:file-backup-when}).
The document has no name | Opens the Save panel; the rest is as for
 | the menu command.
The file cannot be written | The box "Save failed" with the reason;
 | the revision is put back.
.end
After a good save the message line shows "Saved" and the name.  The
Working Directory field of the File panels is not used and not changed by
{k:Ctrl-S}: the file goes where the document came from, which may be a
different place from the directory that the Save panel proposes if another
directory has been used on a File panel since.  The Autosave of the Editor
does the same as {k:Ctrl-S} each time its interval has passed, but makes no
backup ({n:file-backup-autosave}).
.caution The Save panel writes to the Working Directory plus the Document
  Name, and {k:Ctrl-S} writes to the place the document has.  After a Retrieve
  or a Delete in another directory the two can differ.  To be sure where the
  file goes, read the line "Saved" that follows.
.h3 Where the cursor is when a document is opened [file-cursor-place]
.ix Cursor place, saved with the document
.ix Position, saved with the document
.ix Reopening a document at the place of the last change
.ix Document, where the cursor starts
A document that is opened again shows the place where the last change was
saved: the cursor is on the same line of the same block at the same column,
and the line is on the same row of the screen.  Nothing has to be asked for or
set.  The place is stored in the document file itself, so it goes with the file
when the file is copied, and it is the same when the document is opened from
the menu of documents, from the command line, with Retrieve or from a backup.
.table 31 29
Situation | Where the cursor is
The document was saved with changes | At the place of the cursor when it was
 | saved, by Save, {k:Ctrl-S} or Autosave.
The document was only looked at | At the place of the last saved change:
 | moving the cursor changes nothing, so
 | nothing is written for it, and a
 | document that was not changed is not
 | written again ({n:file-save-quick}).
The document was made with Save As | At the place of the cursor when it was
 | saved under the new name.
The document was never saved with a place | At the start of the text, as always.
The document was made from a template | At the start of the text, even if the
 | template file holds a place.
A backup is opened | At the place that the saved version had.
The document is shorter than it was | At the nearest place that is there: the
 | last block, or the end of the block.
The saved place cannot be read | At the start of the text; the document
 | opens as usual.
.end
The place counts the blocks of the document, so if another program changes the
text before it, the cursor can be a few lines away from the place it left; it
is never outside the document.  A cursor at the start of the text, with
the screen at its top, writes no place at all, and removes the old one.  The
place does not change what is printed or exported, does not change the
revision and does not mark the document as changed (there is no star in
front of the name).
.h3 Save at the edges [file-save-edges]
.ix Save, empty document
.ix Save, read-only file
.ix Save, name of a directory
.table 31 29
Case | Result
An empty document | It is written like any other: the settings and one
 | empty block.
A file that is write protected | "cannot write file" (the file is opened for
 | writing and refused).
A name that has a slash and a | The directory must exist; Save does not make
 directory that does not exist | directories: "cannot write file".
A name that is a directory | "cannot write file".
The Document Name field empty | It is rejected with "No file name
 | specified" and the panel stays open.
A backup name, {c:.backup/name.bak01} | Refused with the box "Save failed" and
 | "A backup file is not saved over: use
 | Restore Backup or give another name"
 | ({n:file-backup-use}), after the question
 | "Overwrite old version on disk?" when the
 | file exists.
Extract Current Section, the cursor | "There is no current section".  (The
 in no section | editor keeps the cursor in a section, so
 | this is not met in normal use.)
.end
.h3 The terminal version and the X11 version [file-save-diff]
.ix Save, terminal and X11
The panel and its keys are the same in the terminal version and in the X11
version.  The checklist key is {k:F2} in both; in the terminal version
{k:Alt-+} is the same key as {k:Keypad +} in the X11 version.
.h3 See [file-save-see]
{X:file-backup} on the backup and {x:file-revision} on the
revision; {x:file-templates-create} on making templates; the
chapter on the Global menu for the settings that a template carries; the
Quit command of the Edit chapter, which offers to save a changed document.

.h2 Retrieve [file-retrieve]
.ix Retrieve
.ix Retrieve File panel
.ix Ctrl-R
.ix F12, File, Retrieve
.ix Opening a file
.ix Merging a file into a document
.ix Insert a file
Retrieve brings a file into the Editor.  In its plainest form it replaces the
document that is being edited by another one.  Its other forms take only a part
of a file (the text, the global settings or the typefaces) and put it into the
open document, and one adds the text of a file to the document after the
current section.  Retrieve combines documents, copies the layout of one
document to another, and restores a backup.
.table 20 40
Way to reach it | Notes
{k:F12}, File, Retrieve | Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:R}.
{k:Ctrl-R} | Opens the same panel from anywhere in the Editor.
.end
The menu hint reads "Retrieve a new document file from the disk [Ctrl-R]".
.h3 Ways to open a document [file-retrieving]
.ix Retrieving a file
.ix Opening a document
.ix Document, opening
A document that has been saved is read back in one of two ways.
.table 24 36
Way | What happens
Document Manager, Edit / Preview /
 Print Document | The Document Manager starts the Editor on
 | the name.  A file that is not a Strata
 | document makes the Editor end with status
 | 1, and the Document Manager shows "The
 | editor ended with status 1".
File, Retrieve ({k:Ctrl-R}) | The panel Retrieve File, described below.
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the Editor started by hand with a name reads the file; if it
cannot be read, the program prints the message and ends with exit status 1.  A
name that does not exist is a new document (see {n:file-new-ways}).
.endif
The file can be a document, a template or a backup.  The Editor does not look
at the extension: any file that is a Strata document is read, and everything
else is rejected with the message "The file is not a document of this
software."  A file
that is damaged, cut short or larger than a document can be is refused with a
message that says so (the table below), and the document that is open is not
changed.  When the file is cut short or damaged after a readable beginning, the
box offers the part that can be read ({n:file-recover}).
.note Retrieve is more than loading a file.  It can bring in only the text of
  a file, or only its settings, or only its typefaces, and it can add the text
  of a file to the document that is being edited.
.pic pictures/file-retrieve.png 5 The Retrieve File panel.
.h3 The Retrieve File panel [file-retrieve-panel]
.ix Retrieve File, fields
.ix Document Name, Retrieve
The panel has three fields.
.table 15 45
Field | Meaning and value
Working Directory | The directory in which the file is looked for; shared
 | by all the File panels ({n:file-workdir}).
 | {k:F2} lists the directories.
Document Name | The name of the file.  It begins with the name of the
 | document that is open, without its directory.  {k:F2}
 | lists the files that end with {c:.doc}, {c:.set} or
 | {c:.bak} (and a number).  The field must not be empty.  If there is no
 | file of the name as typed, the name is tried again with
 | {c:.doc} added, when it has no dot.
Options | What is taken from the file, one of six values (the
 | next section).  The last one is Retrieve & Insert after
 | Current Section, or Retrieve & Insert after Current
 | Block when the document is not structured.  Default:
 | Retrieve Entire Document.
.end
The file may be a document, a template or a backup.  What it is called does
not matter: the file must be a Strata document ({n:file-retrieving}).
.h3 What the Options field takes [file-retrieve-options]
.ix Retrieve Entire Document
.ix Retrieve Body Only
.ix Retrieve Global Settings
.ix Retrieve Typeface Settings Only
.ix Retrieve & Insert after Current Section
.pic pictures/file-retrieve-options.png 4 The list of the Options field of the Retrieve panel.
.table 26 34
Value | Effect on the open document
Retrieve Entire Document | The document is replaced by the file: its text,
 | its settings, its description, author and
 | revision.  The document takes the name of the file.
 | The cursor goes to the place saved in the file
 | ({n:file-cursor-place}).  The new document is
 | not marked as changed.
Retrieve Body Only | The text is replaced by the text of the file.  The
 | settings, the name and the revision of the document
 | stay.  The document is structured or not, as the
 | file is.  The cursor goes to the start.
 | The document is marked as changed.
Retrieve Global Settings | The settings are replaced by those of the file; the
 | text stays.  See the table below for what is
 | settings.  The document is marked as changed.
Retrieve Typeface Settings | Only the typefaces are replaced: the
 Only | assignment of typefaces to the attributes and the
 | page size that goes with it.  The document is
 | marked as changed.
Retrieve Named Block | The named block formats of the file are added to
 Settings Only | the document: a format of the same name is
 | replaced, the others stay.  The text and the other settings do not
 | change.  A file that has no named block format gives the message
 | "The file has no named block settings".
Retrieve & Insert after | The text of the file is added after the current
 Current Section | section and all its subsections.  The first section of
 | the file is a peer of the current section.  Nothing
 | is replaced.  The document is marked as changed.
 | The cursor does not move.
Retrieve & Insert after | The same for a document that is not structured: the
 Current Block | blocks of the file are added after the current block.
.end
.h4 What Retrieve Global Settings replaces [file-retrieve-global]
.ix Retrieve Global Settings, contents
Retrieve Global Settings replaces every setting of the document by the
settings of the file, except two.  The table lists what is replaced and what
is kept.
.table 24 36
Part of the settings | At Retrieve Global Settings
Global formats, page layouts, | Replaced by the file's.
 fonts, marker settings, named |
 block formats, page headers |
 and footers |
Description and author | Replaced by the file's.
Revision | Kept: the document keeps its own revision.
Structured or not | Kept: a structured document stays structured.
.end
.h4 What Retrieve Typeface Settings Only replaces [file-retrieve-typeface]
.ix Typeface settings
The typeface settings are the assignment of fonts to the attributes of the
document (the Global Typeface panel) and the paper size that goes with it.
Nothing else changes: the formats of the Global menu and the page layouts stay
as they were.  To give a document the typefaces of a template and keep the
rest, use this value.
.h3 What happens after the panel [file-retrieve-after]
.ix Retrieve, order of events
.ix Retrieve, unsaved changes
.ix The file has changes that were not saved
When the panel is accepted the Editor works in this order.
.table 4 57
Step | What happens
1 | The Working Directory is remembered and the file is looked for (with
 | {c:.doc} added if the name as typed is not a file).
2 | The file is read.  If it cannot be read, a box with the title "Retrieve
 | failed" shows the reason and nothing else happens.  The document that is
 | open is not touched.
3 | For every value except Retrieve & Insert, if the open document has
 | changes that were not saved, the question "The file has changes that were
 | not saved.  Ignore them?" is asked with Yes and No.  No cancels the
 | command and the document stays as it is.  Retrieve & Insert puts no question.
4 | The work is done, and the message line shows "Retrieved" and the full
 | name of the file.
.end
.pic pictures/file-retrieve-failed.png 4 The message that the file cannot be read.
The reasons in the box "Retrieve failed" are these.
.table 31 29
Reason | Cause
cannot open file | There is no such file, or it cannot be read.  The
 | name has been tried with and without {c:.doc}.
read error | The file was opened but reading failed.
cannot read file | The state of the file could not be read.
The file is not a document of this software. | The file is a text file or
 | another kind of file.  Use Import for text.
The file is version N.  This software reads up to version 3. | The file was
 | written by a newer version of the software.
The file version is N.  This software reads versions 2 to 3. | The version of
 | the file is below the oldest that is read, or it is damaged.
damaged document: a piece of text has no end | The file is a document that
 | is damaged: a piece of text is not ended.
damaged document: it is cut short | The file ends before the end of the
 | document: the start of the text or the end of the document is missing.
 | The disk may have been full when it was saved.  Use the backup ({n:file},
 | {n:file-backup}).
damaged document: a number in the settings has too many digits | A number of
 | the settings has more than seven digits, which no document has.
damaged document: more than N lines or columns | The file has more than 20000
 | lines of settings in its header, more than 2000 lines of settings for one
 | block or more than 5000 columns in one block.
document too large: more than N blocks or markers | The document has more
 | than 500000 blocks and sections or more than 1000000 markers.
file too large (the limit is 32 MB) | The file is larger than any document
 | the program reads.  A document of 4000 pages has about 12 MB.
this is a directory, not a file | The name is the name of a directory.  Choose
 | a file with the list of files.
this is not a regular file | The name is a device or another kind of special file.
file changed while it was read | Another program was writing the file.  Try
 | again when it has finished.
.end
.h3 The part that can be read [file-recover]
.ix Recovery of a damaged document
.ix Damaged document, open the readable part
.ix Cut short, document
A file that is cut short (the disk was full when it was saved) or damaged in
the middle is not lost: what comes before the damage can be read.  When
{b:Retrieve}, the opening of a document from the menu of documents or the
Editor started on a file finds such a file, the box that names the reason also
says how many blocks and sections, and how much of the file, can be read, and
prompts
{c:Open the part that can be read?}  {k:Y} opens it; {k:N} and {k:Esc} do not
open anything ({k:Enter} does not answer).  The part is opened as a new
document with the name {c:NAME.recovered.doc} (or {c:NAME.recovered2.doc} and so
on when that name is taken), in the directory of the file or, when that directory
cannot be written, in the working directory.  It is never the name of the
file, so that saving cannot write over the damaged file, which is never
changed, moved or removed.  The document is marked as changed, its end is
supplied, and the message line shows
{c:Recovered N blocks of NAME; the original is not changed}.

Only what is complete is kept: every block and section before the damage, and
of a block that is cut in the middle the columns that are whole.  A bad place
in the middle of a file stops the reading there: the Editor does not look for
the place where the file goes on, so what stands after the bad place is not in
the part.  A file that is not a document, that is larger than 32 MB, that has
more blocks, markers or lines than the limits allow, a directory or a device is
refused as before and no question is put.  Compare Document Versions does not
offer it: the file is rejected with the message; open the file in the Editor
to recover it, save the recovered document and compare that.  From the command
line, the two print commands refuse a damaged file; with
{c:--recover} they print the part that can be read and say so on the error
output.
.h3 Retrieve at the edges [file-retrieve-edges]
.ix Retrieve, empty file
.ix Retrieve, the document being edited
.ix Retrieve, structured and unstructured
.table 31 29
Case | Result
The file is the document that is open | It is read again.  With Retrieve Entire
 | Document, the unsaved changes are
 | thrown away (after the question); this
 | is the way to go back to the saved
 | version.
A file with no text (a template made by | Retrieve Body Only leaves the document
 Save Global Settings Only) | with one empty section and block;
 | Retrieve & Insert adds nothing.
A template ({c:.set}) or a backup | Read like any document.
 ({c:.bak01}, in {c:.backup}) |
.end
.h3 Using Retrieve [file-retrieve-use]
.ix Retrieve, examples
.ix Combining documents
.ix Copying the layout of a document
.table 29 31
To do this | Use
Open another document | Retrieve Entire Document.
Go back to the version on the disk | Retrieve Entire Document with the name of
 | the document.
Put a standard chapter into a document | Retrieve & Insert after Current
 | Section, with the cursor in the
 | section before it.
Reuse the text of a form letter in a | Retrieve Body Only, with the form letter.
 document that has its own layout |
Give a document the layout of a template | Retrieve Global Settings, with it.
Give a document the typefaces of a template | Retrieve Typeface Settings Only.
Restore a backup | The entry Restore Backup of the Document
 | Manager ({n:file-backup-use}).
.end
.h3 See [file-retrieve-see]
{X:file-retrieving} on the other ways to open a document, {x:file-checklist} on {k:F2}, {x:file-templates} on templates, and
{x:file-backup} on backups.  The Document Manager has its own
Edit / Preview / Print Document, which starts the Editor on a file.
